WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.000
Let’s say you have this cuboid shaped box

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.440
placed on the table!

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.280
Try pushing it a little.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.799
You will require some force to push it forward.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.720
Now place this same box on top of cylindrical

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.770
shaped pencils and push it.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.610
What do you notice?

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.910
You will notice that the force required by

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.210
you to push it to the same point as before

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.980
will be lesser!!

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.980
Why is that?

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.100
Do the pencils reduce the friction between

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.590
the box and the table?

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:33.590
Absolutely!

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.320
But it’s not about the pencils; it’s about

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.640
the SHAPE of the pencils.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.570
They are cylindrical…

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.400
Assume you are travelling to another city

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.830
and you are carrying luggage weighing around

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:47.100
25 kilograms.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.380
If you are unable to carry the bag, the only

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.620
option you have is to drag it on the floor!

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.440
But you will still require a lot of effort

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.920
to drag your bag.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.559
But what if your luggage had wheels under it?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.990
Won’t it be FAR easier to carry it around?

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.000
Yes it will be easier!

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.070
But the question is why?

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:10.301
Why is it easier to roll a box over cylindrical

00:01:10.301 --> 00:01:13.670
pencils relative to pushing it on a flat surface?

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:17.060
Why is it easier to carry the bag around when

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.020
it has rollers under it?

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:20.210
Hold on to this thought!

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.580
I’ll come back to it.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.160
Before we discuss the above situations,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.270
we need to understand what SLIDING FRICTION is.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.020
The concept is simple!

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.416
It is the frictional force which opposes the

00:01:32.416 --> 00:01:35.030
relative sliding motion between the two surfaces

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:39.030
in contact ! What does this mean in simple words!

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.900
It simply means that if an object is in motion

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.119
on a surface, the frictional force that is

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:47.350
offered by the surface is called the SLIDING FRICTION.

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.090
The key words here are SLIDING MOTION!

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.960
The two surfaces have to be in relative motion

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.330
with each other!

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.740
In our example, when a box is moving on the

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.720
table, we say that the box is in relative

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.860
motion with the table.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.650
The resistive force applied by the table when

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.440
the box is moving is called sliding friction!

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.990
Remember, the objects have to be in relative motion!

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.060
Because there’s movement involved, this

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:19.130
force is also called KINETIC FRICTION .

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:21.520
Now consider the example of the luggage in

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.970
which we don’t have the wheels under it.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.080
When we move the luggage, what will the resistive

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.560
force applied by the ground be called?

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:31.980
Yes, sliding friction!

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.060
But what happens when we attach circular WHEELS

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.340
at the bottom of the luggage?

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.610
Yes, the friction gets reduced to a great extent.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.700
This type of friction which arises when an

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.431
object is rolled on a surface is called ROLLING

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.260
FRICTION.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.590
It is a force associated with rotational movement!

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.760
Now can you tell me by pure intuition about

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.740
which force will be lesser?

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.840
Sliding friction or rolling friction?

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.300
[pause]

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.980
Yes, rolling friction is usually lesser than

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.400
the one associated with sliding friction!

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.200
And that’s why attaching wheels at the bottom

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.910
makes it easier to move the object around!

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:18.383
And similarly, moving the box on top of the

00:03:18.383 --> 00:03:21.209
cylindrical pencils is also easier!

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:23.874
Can you think of more examples where the concept

00:03:23.874 --> 00:03:26.150
of rolling friction is used to reduce the

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:27.379
friction?

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:29.960
Yes, tyres of your car!

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.980
When the car is in motion, the ground applies

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.390
a force of resistance which is nothing but

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.230
rolling friction on the tyres!

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.380
Do you remember seeing ball bearings?

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.180
They are spherical metal balls used in many

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.500
mechanical structures which have moving parts.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.820
They’re generally made of stainless steel,

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.150
brass or ceramic!

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:53.680
They reduce friction to a great extent which

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.209
in turn reduces the wear and tear of machine

00:03:56.209 --> 00:03:57.430
parts!

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.849
So we got the concept of Sliding and Rolling friction!

00:04:00.849 --> 00:04:03.700
There’s another interesting type of friction

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:05.060
we need to know about!

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.640
Will the force of friction come into play

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.700
only when the two things are in relative motion?

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:13.379
Not necessarily!

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.140
To know which kind of friction I’m talking

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.060
about, watch our next video!

